<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <RootNamespace>Lily.Docs</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Lily.Docs</AssemblyName>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>false</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The two Texinfo packages are PINNED AT ONE VERSION AND MOVE TOGETHER. Bumping
    one without the other is the mistake this comment exists to prevent (the same
    pin discipline the CodeBrix.LilyScheme reference carries). Texinfo2Pdf brings
    Texinfo2Html transitively; it is named anyway so the pin is visible in one
    place and so neither can drift.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="CodeBrix.Texinfo2Html.MitLicenseForever" Version="1.0.233.879" />
    <PackageReference Include="CodeBrix.Texinfo2Pdf.MitLicenseForever" Version="1.0.233.879" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ⚠ LINUX NATIVE ASSETS - LOAD-BEARING, AND NOT TO BE "TIDIED AWAY".

    This tool rasterizes the engraved SVG pictures through
    CodeBrix.Texinfo2Pdf -> CodeBrix.PdfDocCreate.Html2Pdf -> SkiaSharp, and on Linux
    that needs a SkiaSharp native-assets package IN THIS TOOL'S OWN OUTPUT DIRECTORY.
    Neither Texinfo2Pdf nor Html2Pdf declares one, DELIBERATELY: two mutually exclusive
    Linux variants exist (SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.Linux and
    SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.Linux.NoDependencies) and only the consuming application may
    choose between them, so declaring one in a library would force that choice on every
    consumer. Do NOT read their silence as "not needed".

    //was previously: nothing was declared here, because Html2Pdf 1.0.232.60 still
    //declared SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.Linux.NoDependencies and Lily.Docs picked it up
    //transitively. Html2Pdf 1.0.232.1177 REMOVED that declaration, so the transitive
    //supply is gone and this reference is what replaces it.

    ⚠ WITHOUT IT NOTHING CRASHES - which is exactly why it needs saying. Every SVG
    picture is silently SKIPPED, the manuals render complete and picture-less, and the
    only evidence is one collected PDF-stage warning with the code
    "image.svg.nativemissing". The expected-warnings baselines are what catch it.

    ⚠ THE PLAIN VARIANT IS DELIBERATE, not a coin toss. This PackageReference flows
    transitively through Lily.Shell.Core into Lily.Shell's three Linux heads, and those
    heads already receive the PLAIN variant from CodeBrix.Platform.Runtime.Skia.*.
    Referencing .NoDependencies here would put BOTH variants in every one of those heads,
    which is the one arrangement the Texinfo AGENT-README rules out. One variant, repo-wide.

    ⚠ THIS BELONGS HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE. Lily.Docs is a repo tool that ships nothing
    (IsPackable=false above), so this declares nothing to anybody. CodeBrix.LilyPort
    itself must NEVER carry a native-assets reference - decision D52 keeps the whole
    Texinfo -> Html2Pdf -> SkiaSharp chain out of the shipped package.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.Linux" Version="4.151.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    CodeBrix.LilyPort references its sub-assemblies with PrivateAssets="all" so the
    package ships as one unit, which means the Engine does NOT flow transitively.
    Named directly, exactly as DocsDriver does, for the one thing it needs: reading
    the vendored ly/generate-documentation.ly out of the Engine's embedded
    resources rather than off a source path that only exists in a checkout.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\..\src\CodeBrix.LilyPort\CodeBrix.LilyPort.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\..\src\CodeBrix.LilyPort.Engine\CodeBrix.LilyPort.Engine.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The two GFDL macro files the Internals Reference cannot render without
    (decision D49(a)). Vendored so the tool works without a lilypond checkout;
    byte-identical to ../../../Documentation/en/, which VendoredAssetTests fences.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\assets\en\*.itexi" Link="assets\en\%(Filename)%(Extension)"
          CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Decision D57 (2026-08-19). Two more vendored sets, copied beside the assembly for
    the same reason assets/en is: the tool must run out of its output directory with no
    repository present.

      bib/     the five bibliographies translated ONCE by the BibTeX oracle, plus the
               .bst style program that produced them (reference only — nothing executes
               it). The essay manual cannot render its literature list without three of
               these; upstream makes them at build time and they exist in no checkout.
      staged/  ROADMAP and code-review-checklist.md, which the Contributor's Guide prints
               verbatim. Upstream stages them out of the source tree the same way.

    ⚠ Globbed rather than named so a re-sync that adds a file does not silently fail to
    copy it — the MANIFEST.sha256 gates are what fence the CONTENT.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\assets\bib\*" Link="assets\bib\%(Filename)%(Extension)"
          CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <None Include="..\..\assets\staged\*" Link="assets\staged\%(Filename)%(Extension)"
          CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
